# tcpdrill, and what a connect() that has been told it failed leaves running.
#
# connect() on a blocking socket with SO_SNDTIMEO returns ETIMEDOUT when the
# handshake does not finish in time.  Returning that errno is not the whole of
# the operation: the SYN ladder underneath belongs to NetX Duo, not to the
# wrapper's wait, and nothing about the wait expiring stops it.  Left running
# it does two things that are visible from the far end of the wire --
#
#   * it keeps sending SYNs to a peer the application has given up on, for as
#     long as the retry ladder allows, which on this build is another minute
#     past the errno; and
#   * a SYN+ACK that arrives afterwards finds a socket still in SYN_SENT, and
#     COMPLETES THE HANDSHAKE.  The descriptor the caller was told had failed
#     is then connected, to a peer that believes it has a connection, with
#     nobody reading it.
#
# Neither is observable from the errno, which is why this file is on the wire
# and not in a socket-surface test.
#
# WHY THE FIRST CASE IS HERE.  A blocking connect() is the only directive in
# its case that runs, so nothing is draining the tap device while it does, and
# ARP therefore cannot be answered: an unresolved peer means the SYN never
# leaves and the case measures nothing.  cc00 is an ordinary non-blocking
# connect whose only job is to leave 10.9.9.2 in the ARP cache, and to say so
# out loud rather than have the file depend on some other case having run.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# The peer is reachable and the wire works.  Also: the ARP cache now holds
# 10.9.9.2, which is what the cases below need before they block.
case cc00_the_peer_is_reachable
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 within=1000 mss=1460
rx R seq=0

# RFC 793 3.4 has no opinion here and POSIX only says connect() reports
# ETIMEDOUT.  The requirement is the ordinary one: an operation the caller was
# told had failed has stopped.
#
# 2500 ms of SO_SNDTIMEO covers the first two rungs of the ladder (0 and about
# 1 s) and part of the third, so between one and four SYNs is the whole range
# the timing can produce.  What is asserted is not how many went out before the
# errno but that NONE went out after it: the next rung of an uncancelled ladder
# is due within 2 s of the return, and 3 s of quiet is longer than that.
case cc01_a_timed_out_connect_stops_retransmitting
socket
blocking
opt sndtimeo 2500
connect = ETIMEDOUT min=2000 max=3500
tx S seq=0 mss=1460
txcount 0 3
notx 3000

# And the descriptor cannot be connected behind the caller's back. This arm
# also disables the socket break signal: a finite timeout still has to keep
# time accurately when there is no signal to interleave. The SYN+ACK
# below is the one that was too late: a socket still in SYN_SENT answers it
# with an ACK and is established from that moment, and the application is never
# told, because it was told the opposite two seconds earlier.
#
# Two assertions, one on each side of the socket API.
#
#   * ON THE WIRE, nothing goes out.  An ACK here is the third segment of a
#     handshake and it gives the peer a connection this end will never read.
#     Measured: a cancelled connect answers a late SYN+ACK with silence, not
#     with a reset -- there is no socket left to reset from, and the listen
#     path in nx_tcp_packet_process.c deliberately has no answer for a
#     SYN+ACK.  Silence is therefore what is asserted, and an ACK fails it.
#   * AT THE DESCRIPTOR, recv() says ENOTCONN.  A socket that the late SYN+ACK
#     connected says EWOULDBLOCK instead -- established, nothing to read --
#     which is the same sentence a working stack would never say about a
#     connect that reported ETIMEDOUT.
case cc02_a_late_synack_does_not_connect_the_descriptor
socket
blocking
opt sndtimeo 2500
connect = ETIMEDOUT min=2000 max=3500 break=0
tx S seq=0 mss=1460
txcount 0 3
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
notx 1000
opt rcvtimeo 300
recv 100 = ENOTCONN
